WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.090 --> 00:00:06.700
HOFFMAN: Foreigners coming to America to kill
Americans - we immediately condemn it, and

00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:12.610
we see it as offensive, wrong, illegal, and
we've waged a 20-year war on terrorism against

00:00:12.610 --> 00:00:14.160
this kind of threat.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:18.300
Somehow when it occurs in the United States,
it's something we ignore, we don't want to

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:19.810
come to grips with.

00:00:19.810 --> 00:00:24.590
And in part, that's because of constitutional
freedoms, freedom of expression, freedom of

00:00:24.590 --> 00:00:25.590
assembly.

00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:27.530
But we have to understand that words matter.

00:00:27.530 --> 00:00:32.880
And that words can be weaponized, that as
we've seen, unfortunately, in the digital

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:36.630
era, be very quickly and easily transformed
into wanton outright violence

00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:47.400
SIERRA: Last month, on a bright day in the
Capitol, President Biden used a phrase that

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:51.309
no previous president had used in an inaugural
address.

00:00:51.309 --> 00:00:52.309
White supremacy.

00:00:52.309 --> 00:00:56.940
President Joe Biden: A rise in political extremism,
white supremacy, domestic terrorism.

00:00:56.940 --> 00:01:02.309
SIERRA: His words were delivered in the very
spot where just one week earlier a mob of

00:01:02.309 --> 00:01:07.180
insurrectionists stormed the Capitol at the
behest of President Donald Trump.

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:13.220
The riot was not exclusively a white supremacist
event, but it did bring together extremists

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:19.119
from a wide array of white nationalist and
white supremacist groups - many openly carrying

00:01:19.119 --> 00:01:22.700
flags and symbols in support of their movements.

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:27.700
One of the most haunting images of that day
was that of the confederate flag being lofted

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:30.270
in the halls of the Capitol building.

00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:36.610
For years, the United States has devoted enormous
resources to fighting transnational terrorism,

00:01:36.610 --> 00:01:42.380
but until now, the threat of homegrown, white
supremacist terror has not received the same

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:43.590
focus.

00:01:43.590 --> 00:01:49.310
Even after multiple mass shootings, even as
the FBI and the Department of Homeland Security

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:53.219
designated it as the most lethal domestic
threat.

00:01:53.219 --> 00:01:55.640
But now, that seems to be changing.

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:58.909
I'm Gabrielle Sierra, and this is Why It Matters.

00:01:58.909 --> 00:02:03.330
Today, white nationalism, white supremacy,
and the fight to come.

00:02:03.330 --> 00:02:04.330
The New Yorker: "USA, USA…"

00:02:04.330 --> 00:02:06.880
CBS NEWS: The situation has reached a dire
point where the U.S Capitol is now in lockdown,

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:09.350
both the House and Senate.

00:02:09.350 --> 00:02:19.810
CBS NEWS: We are learning more about the Capitol
police officer who died of the injuries from

00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:20.810
the attack.

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:22.379
42-year-old Brian Sicknick served his country
in Iraq.

00:02:22.379 --> 00:02:23.930
Arnold Schwarzenegger: I grew up in Austria.

00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:28.280
I'm very aware of Kristallnacht, or the Night
of Broken Glass.

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:35.209
It was a night of rampage against the Jews
carried out in 1938 by the Nazi equivalent

00:02:35.209 --> 00:02:37.260
of the Proud Boys.

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:41.959
Wednesday was the Day of Broken Glass right
here in the United States.

00:02:41.959 --> 00:02:49.640
SIERRA: So, to start with, I think it's important
to have a definition.

00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:53.060
What is white nationalism?

00:02:53.060 --> 00:03:01.950
HOFFMAN: White nationalism, I would define,
is an overarching concept that embraces the

00:03:01.950 --> 00:03:04.310
idea of white supremacism.

00:03:04.310 --> 00:03:06.409
SIERRA: This is Bruce Hoffman.

00:03:06.409 --> 00:03:10.840
He's a Senior Fellow for counterterrorism
and homeland security at the Council on Foreign

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:13.819
Relations and a leading expert on terrorism.

00:03:13.819 --> 00:03:20.489
In 2014 he was appointed by Congress to lead
a review of the FBI's response to 9/11.

00:03:20.489 --> 00:03:27.130
HOFFMAN: It embraces a very strident xenophobic
or anti-immigrant position.

00:03:27.130 --> 00:03:30.879
It is hostile to all those who are deemed,
quote-unquote, "the other."

00:03:30.879 --> 00:03:36.069
In other words, all these strangers, all these
new immigrants, are taking what is "ours,"

00:03:36.069 --> 00:03:38.879
but not just "ours" in any kind of material
sense.

00:03:38.879 --> 00:03:39.879
"Ours" indeed is a birthright.

00:03:39.879 --> 00:03:47.909
The goal of a white nationalist, I would argue,
is to rally as many people of his or her race

00:03:47.909 --> 00:03:56.250
into not just accepting but emulating and
adopting their own exclusionary, discriminatory

00:03:56.250 --> 00:03:57.879
xenophobic views.

00:03:57.879 --> 00:04:03.780
SIERRA: The topic of white nationalism and
white supremacist extremism is complex.

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:08.599
And it lies in the shadow of the far larger
problem of racism itself.

00:04:08.599 --> 00:04:12.870
As you might expect with such a complicated
issue, there is debate about nomenclature.

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:16.780
MILLER-IDRISS: I'm going to use the term white
supremacist extremism and then tell you why

00:04:16.780 --> 00:04:19.810
I think it's better than white nationalism.

00:04:19.810 --> 00:04:25.630
SIERRA: This is Cynthia Miller-Idris, a sociologist
at American University in the School of Public

00:04:25.630 --> 00:04:31.600
Affairs and the director of the Polarization
and Extremism Research and Innovation Lab,

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:32.600
or PERIL.

00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:37.860
MILLER-IDRISS: So white supremacist extremism
is an ideology that believes in a hierarchy

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:44.280
of superiority and inferiority, that is racial,
that it puts white people, "white civilizations,"

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:47.620
as they often use the term, above all others.

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:49.590
It's a global ideology.

00:04:49.590 --> 00:04:54.560
And that global ideology rests on a conspiracy
theory that is called the Great Replacement

00:04:54.560 --> 00:05:00.820
that believes that white civilizations are
being deliberately eradicated through demographic

00:05:00.820 --> 00:05:06.889
change and immigration at the hands of usually
Jews and Muslims.

00:05:06.889 --> 00:05:16.160
Vox: America was built by and for the white
Christian people in this nation.

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:18.230
CNN: We're staring down the barrel of a gun
here in white America.

00:05:18.230 --> 00:05:21.160
We have the possibility of becoming a minority
in our own country...a possibility.

00:05:21.160 --> 00:05:22.160
EURONEWS: I say immigrants, I'm actually using
the wrong word.

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:23.160
They are actually conquerors, and that's the
thing that we need to understand.

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:28.530
Most white people are handing them the keys
to our country.

00:05:28.530 --> 00:05:33.530
MILLER-IDRISS: White supremacist extremists,
they have specific policy proposals in mind,

00:05:33.530 --> 00:05:37.170
but essentially, they want to restore a white
civilization.

00:05:37.170 --> 00:05:43.051
They want to deport or what they sometimes
call in softer language re-migrate people

00:05:43.051 --> 00:05:46.770
who are non-white back to their countries
of origin.

00:05:46.770 --> 00:05:50.310
So white nationalism, which some people use,
has two problems.

00:05:50.310 --> 00:05:55.819
One is that it can sometimes have the effect
of softening what is really extremist, and

00:05:55.819 --> 00:06:01.200
sometimes even terrorist behavior and making
it sound like it's just overblown patriotism.

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:07.060
And it also sort of has the effect of making
it seem like it's a national problem when

00:06:07.060 --> 00:06:09.280
I think it is clearly a global problem.

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:14.810
SIERRA: If you have spent any time on the
internet or listening to the news or podcasts

00:06:14.810 --> 00:06:19.030
about this problem, you will know that there
are lots of groups.

00:06:19.030 --> 00:06:24.310
So, there are dozens of militias, neo-Nazi
organizations, and groups with a specific

00:06:24.310 --> 00:06:26.039
emphasis on hate.

00:06:26.039 --> 00:06:31.650
There are the Oath Keepers and the Three Percenters,
and the Proud Boys, the self-defined "western

00:06:31.650 --> 00:06:36.919
chauvinists," who gained some fame after President
Trump told them to "stand back and stand by."

00:06:36.919 --> 00:06:41.720
And then, there are other groups that overlap
into this far-right extremist space.

00:06:41.720 --> 00:06:46.570
There's QAnon, a conspiracy theory that features
Satan-worshipping pedophiles controlling our

00:06:46.570 --> 00:06:47.570
government.

00:06:47.570 --> 00:06:52.001
There are concepts like the "boogaloo," a
coded reference to a second civil war, and

00:06:52.001 --> 00:06:57.509
"incels," a rallying idea for men with deeply
misogynistic and violent views.

00:06:57.509 --> 00:07:01.790
The deeper you dig, the more terms and movements
you uncover.

00:07:01.790 --> 00:07:07.949
SIERRA: Do you think that one problem with
assessing all this is just the sheer number

00:07:07.949 --> 00:07:09.290
of groups?

00:07:09.290 --> 00:07:12.660
To what degree are these groups all part of
the same movement?

00:07:12.660 --> 00:07:15.720
And what makes it so difficult to pin this
down?

00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:20.590
HOFFMAN: It's very hard to get a handle on
how many individuals are involved in these

00:07:20.590 --> 00:07:24.280
types of movements because a lot of it is
done anonymously.

00:07:24.280 --> 00:07:28.460
And that's one of the benefits of social media,
the internet, is you can be completely anonymous;

00:07:28.460 --> 00:07:33.770
you could have avatars or monikers that don't
reveal your true identity.

00:07:33.770 --> 00:07:38.010
But there's another element to this is that
we're not talking about organizations, we're

00:07:38.010 --> 00:07:42.220
talking about networks, and networks that
often have overlapping memberships.

00:07:42.220 --> 00:07:48.030
I mean, we may like to create our boxes of
the Three Percenters or the Oath Keepers,

00:07:48.030 --> 00:07:53.139
or the Proud Boys, or the QAnon, and so on,
what we fail to realize that you don't get

00:07:53.139 --> 00:07:54.639
a membership card.

00:07:54.639 --> 00:07:59.900
SIERRA: There may not be membership cards,
but there is a basic idea shared across all

00:07:59.900 --> 00:08:01.830
white nationalist groups.

00:08:01.830 --> 00:08:06.629
And that's the idea that white Americans are
the only legitimate Americans.

00:08:06.629 --> 00:08:12.449
Problems, whether personal, national, or imagined,
are blamed on non-white Americans.

00:08:12.449 --> 00:08:19.340
MILLER-IDRISS: So the way I think of it, it's
a spectrum of far-right groups that share

00:08:19.340 --> 00:08:25.909
both anti-democratic ideals, beliefs, and
practices, hierarchies of beliefs about superiority

00:08:25.909 --> 00:08:31.659
and inferiority, and a willingness to use
violence, to enact their political goals.

00:08:31.659 --> 00:08:37.140
Combined with people who are called accelerationist,
or have fantasies about a collapse of society,

00:08:37.140 --> 00:08:42.820
the desire to bring about a civil war, or
an insurrection or chaos for the sake of chaos,

00:08:42.820 --> 00:08:47.760
so that there'll be a collapse and apocalyptic
kind of end times, and then rebirth into a

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:48.760
new civilization.

00:08:48.760 --> 00:08:53.959
HOFFMAN: And the trouble is because their
agendas are so overlapping in many respects,

00:08:53.959 --> 00:08:56.120
people subscribe to a variety of them.

00:08:56.120 --> 00:09:00.390
So we're talking about something that is much
more amorphous and much more diffuse than

00:09:00.390 --> 00:09:01.720
we've ever seen in the past.

00:09:01.720 --> 00:09:07.130
And, of course, this challenges the very foundation
of how we've always understood counterterrorism

00:09:07.130 --> 00:09:08.870
by focusing on the organizations.

00:09:08.870 --> 00:09:13.350
This is why the U.S. State Department, for
instance, has a foreign terrorist organization

00:09:13.350 --> 00:09:14.440
designation.

00:09:14.440 --> 00:09:18.130
Because you can identify the organization,
you can identify the leader, and you can get

00:09:18.130 --> 00:09:20.880
a sense of how many members, and then you
can sanction it.

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:26.660
We're talking about something that is much
more difficult to tangibly understand.

00:09:26.660 --> 00:09:32.390
And therefore, this has worked to their advantage
and also slowed our efforts not just to understand

00:09:32.390 --> 00:09:34.160
but to legislate against it.

00:09:34.160 --> 00:09:37.670
MILLER-IDRISS: There's definitely not a unified
movement, even though they have tried to.

00:09:37.670 --> 00:09:43.790
So, in the U.S., the most obvious moment when
they deliberately tried to unify the movement

00:09:43.790 --> 00:09:48.490
was in Charlottesville in 2017, when they
held a rally that a lot of people have forgotten,

00:09:48.490 --> 00:09:50.110
that was actually called Unite the Right.

00:09:50.110 --> 00:09:51.240
CNN: Jews will not replace us.

00:09:51.240 --> 00:09:52.240
Jews will not replace us.

00:09:52.240 --> 00:09:55.279
CBS: Today's Unite the Right rally, which
is expected to draw thousands of white nationalists,

00:09:55.279 --> 00:09:56.279
neo-
Nazis.

00:09:56.279 --> 00:09:58.620
CNN: They carry torches a nod to the torches
like Nazi rallies of Adolph Hitler.

00:09:58.620 --> 00:09:59.620
They chanted, 'Jews will not replace us.'

00:09:59.620 --> 00:10:03.602
ABC News: It was a weekend of street battles
and stark displays of racism.

00:10:03.602 --> 00:10:14.269
Tonight, we examine the hateful groups that
now appear to be banding together.

00:10:14.269 --> 00:10:19.350
MILLER-IDRISS: That was the whole intent of
that rally was to try to bring together what

00:10:19.350 --> 00:10:25.020
had been a far-right spectrum that was infighting,
that was at odds with each other, that was

00:10:25.020 --> 00:10:29.890
deeply fragmented, and try to unite them all,
under one banner and see if they could be

00:10:29.890 --> 00:10:34.360
a stronger force in American society and in
American politics.

00:10:34.360 --> 00:10:35.360
That failed.

00:10:35.360 --> 00:10:38.520
They fragmented just as badly, if not worse
afterward.

00:10:38.520 --> 00:10:45.110
But I think what we then saw was three and
a half years of the mainstreaming and normalization

00:10:45.110 --> 00:10:49.709
of right-wing, far-right, whatever you want
to call it, and then they unified around this

00:10:49.709 --> 00:10:52.700
sort of disinformation landscape around the
election.

00:10:52.700 --> 00:10:59.470
And so what we saw then, in the US, January
6, was a real uniting of the right, unfortunately,

00:10:59.470 --> 00:11:04.250
a broad spectrum of far-right groups that
came together to enact violence.

00:11:04.250 --> 00:11:10.190
So really, those goals that these groups had
forged back in 2017 really did come to fruition

00:11:10.190 --> 00:11:11.769
in 2021.

00:11:11.769 --> 00:11:16.570
SIERRA: As experts analyzed evidence from
the Capitol attack, they found that members

00:11:16.570 --> 00:11:23.050
of more than a dozen of these groups had participated,
including Qanon adherents, militia members,

00:11:23.050 --> 00:11:26.400
Proud Boys, and white supremacist organizations.

00:11:26.400 --> 00:11:31.680
The spectacle of these different groups, uniting
around an attempt to overturn an election,

00:11:31.680 --> 00:11:33.680
shocked many across the country.

00:11:33.680 --> 00:11:37.210
But the problem has been centuries in the
making.

00:11:37.210 --> 00:11:39.519
HOFFMAN: It goes way back, of course.

00:11:39.519 --> 00:11:42.780
One could argue that it goes back to even
before the founding of the Republic, of course,

00:11:42.780 --> 00:11:46.829
when slavery was introduced to the United
States in 1619.

00:11:46.829 --> 00:11:51.649
One can trace it certainly, to the Know Nothing
movements that arose in the United States

00:11:51.649 --> 00:11:56.519
at the beginning of the 19th century, which
sought to target, in particular, Irish Catholic

00:11:56.519 --> 00:11:59.549
immigrants to the urban centers of the Northeast.

00:11:59.549 --> 00:12:04.389
It certainly emerged in the period of reconstruction
with the emergence of the Ku Klux Klan.

00:12:04.389 --> 00:12:11.240
It gathered new momentum in the 1920s, especially
when very stringent and strict immigration

00:12:11.240 --> 00:12:15.600
laws were passed precisely to keep immigrants
from eastern and southern Europe from coming

00:12:15.600 --> 00:12:20.040
to the United States who were seen as not
just polluting the United States and poisoning

00:12:20.040 --> 00:12:25.560
its white Anglo-Saxon Christian purity, but
I think significantly for 2021, were seen

00:12:25.560 --> 00:12:29.070
as bringing disease and plagues with them.

00:12:29.070 --> 00:12:34.120
And of course, we've seen, then, it resurface
in the 1950s, in the 1960s.

00:12:34.120 --> 00:12:40.620
But I would say the launch point for what
we see in the 21st century really occurred

00:12:40.620 --> 00:12:46.920
in the late 1970s - early 1980s, when you
had, I think, a remarkably similar constellation

00:12:46.920 --> 00:12:47.920
of events.

00:12:47.920 --> 00:12:53.320
You had, firstly, many Americans who were
tired of a prolonged overseas military expedition

00:12:53.320 --> 00:12:59.240
and engagement in a far-off land that was
in Vietnam and the U.S. wars in Indochina.

00:12:59.240 --> 00:13:03.829
That because of the prolongation of those
wars, and what they saw was the defeat where

00:13:03.829 --> 00:13:08.050
the United States, after the considerable
sacrifice of blood and treasure, just picked

00:13:08.050 --> 00:13:12.740
up and left this profound disillusionment
with elected leadership.

00:13:12.740 --> 00:13:17.760
It was a time also coming off of profound
economic instability and very high inflation

00:13:17.760 --> 00:13:25.570
rates that created fears of jobs disappearing
overseas, for example, of really the diminishment

00:13:25.570 --> 00:13:29.800
of blue-collar employment opportunities in
the United States and the emergence of the

00:13:29.800 --> 00:13:31.780
Rust Belt as it were.

00:13:31.780 --> 00:13:38.190
And all those factors combined to give rise
to what was the first attempts to really integrate

00:13:38.190 --> 00:13:45.300
and coordinate and provide an organizational
superstructure to the various strands of white

00:13:45.300 --> 00:13:52.870
nationalism, white supremacism, racism, extreme
anti-government sentiment, belief in no veracity

00:13:52.870 --> 00:13:57.950
or no power of the government above the county
level, anti-semitism and so on.

00:13:57.950 --> 00:14:02.070
And we see many stark parallels today to that
previous era.

00:14:02.070 --> 00:14:07.699
SIERRA: The period of the 1970s and 80s may
not be the most notorious moment in the history

00:14:07.699 --> 00:14:09.839
of American racial terror.

00:14:09.839 --> 00:14:15.310
But Bruce points it out, not only because
of the stark parallels to our own time but

00:14:15.310 --> 00:14:20.980
also because innovations among white supremacists
then sowed the seeds of the problem as it

00:14:20.980 --> 00:14:22.570
exists now.

00:14:22.570 --> 00:14:29.389
HOFFMAN: The advent of lone wolves or lone
actor terrorism, and also online radicalization

00:14:29.389 --> 00:14:36.300
and recruitment, both have their roots in
1983 and 1984, when American white supremacists

00:14:36.300 --> 00:14:41.579
championed a strategy of what they called
leaderless resistance or "autonomous phantom

00:14:41.579 --> 00:14:42.810
cell units."

00:14:42.810 --> 00:14:47.320
In other words, the FBI in that era was so
successful in penetrating and undermining,

00:14:47.320 --> 00:14:51.899
and neutralizing these groups that the white
supremacists and white nationalists of that

00:14:51.899 --> 00:14:53.910
era decided we need a new strategy.

00:14:53.910 --> 00:14:59.730
SIERRA: By decentralizing, these groups made
it much harder for law enforcement to track,

00:14:59.730 --> 00:15:01.900
infiltrate, and break them up.

00:15:01.900 --> 00:15:07.410
By forgoing hierarchy and instead relying
on individual actors, a threat could emerge

00:15:07.410 --> 00:15:09.500
from anywhere at any time.

00:15:09.500 --> 00:15:14.279
HOFFMAN: The second innovation is that at
the time, the white supremacists in the United

00:15:14.279 --> 00:15:19.420
States were deliberately trying to forge international
links, particularly with their like-minded

00:15:19.420 --> 00:15:23.570
hatemongers in Canada and what was then West
Germany.

00:15:23.570 --> 00:15:26.980
The problem was, every time they sent their
hate literature through the U.S. mail, that

00:15:26.980 --> 00:15:31.560
became a federal offense, and they were vulnerable
to being arrested for sending hate mail through

00:15:31.560 --> 00:15:33.420
the U.S. Postal Service.

00:15:33.420 --> 00:15:38.470
So that's why, in fact, the same individual
Louis Beam, who came up, or adopted, or popularized

00:15:38.470 --> 00:15:44.589
the notion of leaderless resistance, in 1984,
came up with the idea of using at the time

00:15:44.589 --> 00:15:50.810
state of the art technology, now very primitive
communications technologies; basically, modems

00:15:50.810 --> 00:15:53.130
that operated 48 to 84 baud.

00:15:53.130 --> 00:15:58.100
I mean, this is like, infinitesimally slower
than anything we have today.

00:15:58.100 --> 00:16:02.579
Using computers that back then cost around
three or four thousand dollars in today's

00:16:02.579 --> 00:16:05.699
money that had 48,000 memory.

00:16:05.699 --> 00:16:10.120
But nonetheless, using these modems and these
bulletin boards, they were able to obviate

00:16:10.120 --> 00:16:14.561
US Postal regulations that would have put
them in jail, but even more importantly, frustrate

00:16:14.561 --> 00:16:19.560
the efforts of the FBI and the authorities
to monitor them, to surveil them, because

00:16:19.560 --> 00:16:23.620
of course, technologically, the U.S. government
was far less sophisticated than the private

00:16:23.620 --> 00:16:24.620
sector.

00:16:24.620 --> 00:16:29.130
And what we see today is that something they
could never have dreamt of in the 1980s, the

00:16:29.130 --> 00:16:34.029
power of social media platforms, where you
have thousands, tens of thousands, hundreds

00:16:34.029 --> 00:16:40.279
of thousands potential friends, associates,
individuals in your circle or network that

00:16:40.279 --> 00:16:44.130
you can reach instantaneously at very low
cost.

00:16:44.130 --> 00:16:50.620
And, of course, radicalize, inspire, motivate
and animate them to commit acts of violence.

00:16:50.620 --> 00:16:53.790
MILLER-IDRISS: I mean, this isn't created
by social media.

00:16:53.790 --> 00:16:58.329
It isn't created by the ability of these online
ecosystems, but they are underpinning it and

00:16:58.329 --> 00:17:05.850
helping its growth and helping people use
these events as a channel for recruitment

00:17:05.850 --> 00:17:08.559
for radicalization and further mobilization.

00:17:08.559 --> 00:17:13.390
There is still a really important component
to it, the real-life kinds of gatherings.

00:17:13.390 --> 00:17:17.160
It's important that this was symbolically
at the Capitol, right?

00:17:17.160 --> 00:17:19.250
But it was also live-streamed.

00:17:19.250 --> 00:17:22.549
There was live chatter on chat fora.

00:17:22.549 --> 00:17:29.260
There were many different ways that the performance
of it also relied on this online ecosystem

00:17:29.260 --> 00:17:33.950
as well as all the planning that goes on prior
to something like this happening, relying

00:17:33.950 --> 00:17:40.420
on encrypted apps and chat rooms, and sometimes
very public social media posts that made threats.

00:17:40.420 --> 00:17:44.120
SIERRA: Social media plays a key role at every
stage.

00:17:44.120 --> 00:17:50.740
It has created an unprecedented level of disinformation
and polarization the world over - and this

00:17:50.740 --> 00:17:54.940
atmosphere is key for recruitment to extremist
ideologies.

00:17:54.940 --> 00:18:00.870
It then facilitates bonds between extremists
despite geographical distance, allowing them

00:18:00.870 --> 00:18:05.470
to talk, refine their hate, and articulate
their goals.

00:18:05.470 --> 00:18:09.920
In the lead-up to an attack, and even during,
it helps them plan and coordinate.

00:18:09.920 --> 00:18:16.400
Finally, it allows them to broadcast the attack
live, furthering their recruitment and accelerationist

00:18:16.400 --> 00:18:17.610
goals.

00:18:17.610 --> 00:18:21.470
The Capital was not the first live-streamed
white extremist attack.

00:18:21.470 --> 00:18:25.760
In fact, by January 6, the technique had been
refined for years.

00:18:25.760 --> 00:18:30.880
One of the most infamous examples happened
in 2019 in Christchurch, New Zealand.

00:18:30.880 --> 00:18:39.180
CBS NEWS: A racist, terror attack in New Zealand,
targeting two mosques, leaving this morning

00:18:39.180 --> 00:18:43.610
at least 49 people dead, and many more wounded.

00:18:43.610 --> 00:18:48.770
ABC NEWS: The man who was claiming responsibility
and charged with murder is an extreme right-wing

00:18:48.770 --> 00:18:51.240
white supremacist who live-streamed the attack,
saying it was aimed at immigrants.

00:18:51.240 --> 00:18:56.540
CAN: Many of those who will have been directly
affected by the shooting may be migrants to

00:18:56.540 --> 00:18:57.620
New Zealand.

00:18:57.620 --> 00:19:00.850
They may even be refugees here.

00:19:00.850 --> 00:19:08.400
They have chosen to make New Zealand their
home, and it is their home.

00:19:08.400 --> 00:19:10.690
They are us.

00:19:10.690 --> 00:19:16.420
MILLER-IDRISS: What happened in Christchurch
was shocking on a lot of levels.

00:19:16.420 --> 00:19:19.299
It was an attack on two mosques.

00:19:19.299 --> 00:19:24.150
People who were peacefully worshiping really
were massacred, you know, gunned down.

00:19:24.150 --> 00:19:30.700
It was live-streamed, and so it began a new
moment in the kind of use of the Internet

00:19:30.700 --> 00:19:35.929
to turn terrorism and to what already had
been for a long time, which is bloody political

00:19:35.929 --> 00:19:38.669
theater, and really bring it to a global stage.

00:19:38.669 --> 00:19:43.720
And then, you know, even though Facebook and
others really tried to pretty quickly take

00:19:43.720 --> 00:19:46.950
down that live stream, it had been downloaded
so many times.

00:19:46.950 --> 00:19:49.480
It was very difficult to get that off.

00:19:49.480 --> 00:19:51.679
There was, you know, music.

00:19:51.679 --> 00:19:53.070
There was a playlist, right?

00:19:53.070 --> 00:19:59.179
It was very much appealing to youth who came
of age kind of, in a very technically highly

00:19:59.179 --> 00:20:04.860
developed online gaming world, where, because
of the use of the GoPro kind of camera that

00:20:04.860 --> 00:20:09.940
allowed for that live streaming to feel more
like an attack in an online game.

00:20:09.940 --> 00:20:15.600
And so that kind of created a whole new way
of communicating terrorist events that I think

00:20:15.600 --> 00:20:24.000
we've seen ever since in terms of using the
live streaming capabilities to try to broadcast

00:20:24.000 --> 00:20:29.289
immediately what was happening and further
terrorize the public in a whole different

00:20:29.289 --> 00:20:30.750
way.

00:20:30.750 --> 00:20:38.659
SIERRA: As shocking as Christchurch was, by
2019, mass-casualty attacks by white extremists

00:20:38.659 --> 00:20:40.730
had become gruesomely common.

00:20:40.730 --> 00:20:47.680
In 2011 a devastating attack in Norway claimed
77 lives and included a manifesto from the

00:20:47.680 --> 00:20:54.060
perpetrator that was enshrined by white supremacists
across the world in the years that followed.

00:20:54.060 --> 00:20:59.880
A 2015 attack on a black church in Charleston,
South Carolina, claimed nine lives.

00:20:59.880 --> 00:21:05.050
In 2018, an attack on the Tree of Life synagogue
in Pittsburgh claimed 11.

00:21:05.050 --> 00:21:09.409
The list goes on, and on, and on.

00:21:09.409 --> 00:21:14.110
As the pattern unfolded, experts began to
warn that domestic terror was emerging as

00:21:14.110 --> 00:21:20.360
a more serious threat than transnational terror
and that U.S. resources long engaged in fighting

00:21:20.360 --> 00:21:23.590
terrorism abroad should be allocated accordingly.

00:21:23.590 --> 00:21:29.700
By 2020, the Department of Homeland Security
had designated violent white supremacy as

00:21:29.700 --> 00:21:33.549
the "most persistent and lethal threat in
the homeland."

00:21:33.549 --> 00:21:35.960
The change was slow to come.

00:21:35.960 --> 00:21:41.039
SIERRA: So, earlier, you know, you mentioned
Al-Qaeda and ISIS.

00:21:41.039 --> 00:21:46.820
So in my lifetime, the word terrorism has
been very strongly associated with Islamic

00:21:46.820 --> 00:21:47.820
extremism.

00:21:47.820 --> 00:21:50.440
So do you think you can tell us a little bit
more about the relationship between those

00:21:50.440 --> 00:21:53.150
movements and white nationalist terrorism?

00:21:53.150 --> 00:21:57.220
HOFFMAN: The parallels between ISIS and the
white supremacists are enormous.

00:21:57.220 --> 00:22:03.630
They're both talking about a halcyon period
in the past of historical greatness that they're

00:22:03.630 --> 00:22:10.690
intent on restoring, and they talk about interlopers,
foreigners, outsiders who are impinging upon

00:22:10.690 --> 00:22:16.890
their customs, their beliefs, their societal
values that need to be countered.

00:22:16.890 --> 00:22:20.240
They're clustering very much inward in an
almost a

00:22:20.240 --> 00:22:23.760
Manichaean "us against them" kind of mentality.

00:22:23.760 --> 00:22:26.559
No, there are, unfortunately, a lot of parallels.

00:22:26.559 --> 00:22:32.559
What I think worries me the most is that there's
a circularity or a feedback loop in that Al-Qaeda

00:22:32.559 --> 00:22:37.100
and ISIS, whether consciously or otherwise,
I would argue, learn from the experience of

00:22:37.100 --> 00:22:41.890
American white supremacist, as I've described
with this concept of lone wolves that harkens

00:22:41.890 --> 00:22:47.060
back to leaderless resistance, which was the
white supremacist term, and the use of radicalization,

00:22:47.060 --> 00:22:51.980
and recruitment using computers, and now subsequently
digital means and social media.

00:22:51.980 --> 00:22:54.710
So I say they learned from the white supremacists.

00:22:54.710 --> 00:22:58.830
But what we're seeing now is the white supremacists
also learning very much in particular, from

00:22:58.830 --> 00:23:04.380
ISIS, and using many of the same tactics,
techniques, and means of radicalization and

00:23:04.380 --> 00:23:09.039
recruitment, that we see ISIS having used
to become so effective in such a short span

00:23:09.039 --> 00:23:10.039
of time.

00:23:10.039 --> 00:23:16.030
SIERRA: The basic goals of ISIS and American
white extremists are similar: they both want

00:23:16.030 --> 00:23:21.559
to drive out the "other" and restore an imaginary
time in which they alone wielded power.

00:23:21.559 --> 00:23:26.929
And these basic motivations are common to
ethnic and nationalist terror all over the

00:23:26.929 --> 00:23:27.929
world.

00:23:27.929 --> 00:23:33.031
In almost every country in Europe, whether
France, Germany, Poland, or Hungary, there

00:23:33.031 --> 00:23:38.540
are extremist groups, and even political parties,
working to push out the "other" and restore

00:23:38.540 --> 00:23:40.390
an imaginary past.

00:23:40.390 --> 00:23:46.130
SIERRA: Do you think that the U.S. national
security apparatus is well-positioned to address

00:23:46.130 --> 00:23:47.130
this threat?

00:23:47.130 --> 00:23:52.360
HOFFMAN: Well, the authorities in the United
States are well-positioned to respond to the

00:23:52.360 --> 00:23:53.360
threat.

00:23:53.360 --> 00:23:58.299
We have enormously effective law enforcement
and intelligence agencies that, when given

00:23:58.299 --> 00:24:04.250
their orders, when given prioritization, directives
from the top, are very effective.

00:24:04.250 --> 00:24:07.289
There's no doubt they've kept us safe for
decades.

00:24:07.289 --> 00:24:11.010
The problem I see is less the agencies and
more the political will.

00:24:11.010 --> 00:24:15.590
And also the national support behind that
political will.

00:24:15.590 --> 00:24:21.630
So there has to be some sort of reckoning,
really, and a deeper discussion that addresses

00:24:21.630 --> 00:24:26.520
the context in the background of how we've
gotten to where we are today, if we're ever

00:24:26.520 --> 00:24:33.640
to make meaningful progress in preventing
the types of outrageous activities that we

00:24:33.640 --> 00:24:36.780
saw in the United States Capitol on January
6.

00:24:36.780 --> 00:24:43.250
I mean, protests that desecrate, that vandalize,
that smear blood and feces on the bastion

00:24:43.250 --> 00:24:48.429
of United States democracy, on probably one
of the most hallowed institutions and buildings

00:24:48.429 --> 00:24:52.770
in this country, I think, indicate that something
has gone wrong somewhere.

00:24:52.770 --> 00:25:00.680
MILLER-IDRISS: I think that the FBI has the
expertise and could shift the resources and

00:25:00.680 --> 00:25:02.590
has been trying to shift the resources.

00:25:02.590 --> 00:25:07.149
The director of the FBI has testified several
times now before Congress about the dilemmas

00:25:07.149 --> 00:25:11.190
they have faced with the imbalance and resources,
what they are supposed to be dedicating their

00:25:11.190 --> 00:25:15.799
time toward, in terms of Islamist terrorism
and international terrorism versus domestic

00:25:15.799 --> 00:25:17.600
extremism and terrorism.

00:25:17.600 --> 00:25:21.340
I think there are a number of other problems,
though, on the law enforcement side, one of

00:25:21.340 --> 00:25:27.010
which is that there is both kind of unconscious
bias and racism that we know is present and

00:25:27.010 --> 00:25:31.190
we have seen throughout the Black Lives Matter
protest is a reaction to police brutality

00:25:31.190 --> 00:25:37.980
that clearly plays a role in the lack of preparatory
response to the January 6 attacks in terms

00:25:37.980 --> 00:25:41.910
of who's deemed a threat and how prepared
they were in riot gear.

00:25:41.910 --> 00:25:46.590
We have anecdote after anecdote of law enforcement
officers, including some who were present

00:25:46.590 --> 00:25:51.740
now, as we're finding out in those attacks
on the Capitol, they're caught for being members

00:25:51.740 --> 00:25:55.730
of white supremacist extremists or other far-right
groups and militias.

00:25:55.730 --> 00:26:00.409
And so you have groups that are deliberately
trying to recruit from law enforcement.

00:26:00.409 --> 00:26:05.320
On the militia side, you have incident after
incident of officers, border control officers,

00:26:05.320 --> 00:26:12.179
nearly 10,000 of them being caught in a racist
Facebook group, you know, sharing racist,

00:26:12.179 --> 00:26:13.860
kind of, information and chats.

00:26:13.860 --> 00:26:15.559
But what we don't have is data.

00:26:15.559 --> 00:26:18.289
And so we don't actually know is this a few
bad apples?

00:26:18.289 --> 00:26:20.409
Or is it a systematic problem?

00:26:20.409 --> 00:26:25.429
There's anecdote after anecdote, and after
a while, so many anecdotes add up to at least

00:26:25.429 --> 00:26:29.520
there should be data available, mandated reporting
from the Department of Defense and the Department

00:26:29.520 --> 00:26:30.610
of Justice.

00:26:30.610 --> 00:26:35.840
SIERRA: Many people don't realize it, but
there is no federal law addressing domestic

00:26:35.840 --> 00:26:36.840
terror.

00:26:36.840 --> 00:26:42.549
Some say this needs to change if the FBI is
going to execute its mission effectively.

00:26:42.549 --> 00:26:47.110
President Biden signaled his support for such
a law in the wake of the Capitol riots and

00:26:47.110 --> 00:26:52.320
has ordered the office of the director of
national intelligence to review the domestic

00:26:52.320 --> 00:26:55.480
terror threat with an eye on future policy.

00:26:55.480 --> 00:27:00.490
Another proposal will allow the FBI to formally
designate domestic terror organizations for

00:27:00.490 --> 00:27:02.120
the first time.

00:27:02.120 --> 00:27:04.890
MILLER-IDRISS: We do need new legislation.

00:27:04.890 --> 00:27:10.520
But I'm also cautious, and I understand the
concerns of civil rights groups about the

00:27:10.520 --> 00:27:12.860
calls for a federal domestic terrorism statute.

00:27:12.860 --> 00:27:15.789
For example, how can we collect and share
the data?

00:27:15.789 --> 00:27:16.909
What's the scope?

00:27:16.909 --> 00:27:17.909
And how do you address it?

00:27:17.909 --> 00:27:23.149
And I understand that it's extremely frustrating
for law enforcement to have to charge people

00:27:23.149 --> 00:27:27.029
on things like weapons charges or something
when actually, it's clear that they were plotting,

00:27:27.029 --> 00:27:30.980
you know, if they don't have the right tools
to charge people with what they're doing.

00:27:30.980 --> 00:27:34.880
But I will say that I think there's a lot
of concern from civil rights groups about

00:27:34.880 --> 00:27:39.430
the violations against Muslim communities
that happened in the post-911 era.

00:27:39.430 --> 00:27:45.070
I think the answer to an injustice like that
is not to repeat the same injustice with other

00:27:45.070 --> 00:27:49.919
citizens to make it equal but to really try
to learn from those mistakes and think how

00:27:49.919 --> 00:27:53.039
we can do better than repeating those same
mistakes?

00:27:53.039 --> 00:27:59.769
HOFFMAN: I think we need new laws that come
to grips more effectively with domestic terrorism.

00:27:59.769 --> 00:28:04.900
Now, senior Department of Justice officials
and those in the FBI, I think, make the entirely

00:28:04.900 --> 00:28:10.470
legitimate and germane and even cogent point
that the existing laws are enough.

00:28:10.470 --> 00:28:15.690
Criminal homicide, vandalism, bombing, arson,
I mean, these are all criminal offenses; we

00:28:15.690 --> 00:28:18.190
don't need additional statutes.

00:28:18.190 --> 00:28:22.970
However, you could make the same point about
hate crimes, which embrace all of those things.

00:28:22.970 --> 00:28:27.610
And we do have separate criminal statutes
for hate crimes that often impose penalties

00:28:27.610 --> 00:28:33.320
in some instances, three or four times greater
than normal, criminal sentences or sanctions.

00:28:33.320 --> 00:28:38.679
Because as a society, we want to single out
hate crimes as being particularly important

00:28:38.679 --> 00:28:44.769
to a country whose motto is "E Pluribus Unum"––"from
many, one," that is talking about the diversity

00:28:44.769 --> 00:28:46.289
of the United States.

00:28:46.289 --> 00:28:51.430
And in that sense, we do need some domestic
terrorism legislation, if only because it

00:28:51.430 --> 00:28:57.929
would close the disparity that we see in the
sentencing, for instance, of persons implicated

00:28:57.929 --> 00:29:02.620
in support of foreign, or commission of violent
acts on behalf of foreign terrorists, and

00:29:02.620 --> 00:29:03.910
those domestic.

00:29:03.910 --> 00:29:08.850
SIERRA: And as the U.S. debates whether it
needs new laws to prosecute domestic terrorists,

00:29:08.850 --> 00:29:15.060
there is a more difficult debate afoot about
how to reign in social media's enormous power

00:29:15.060 --> 00:29:19.300
to amplify hate and aid in the commission
of terrorist acts.

00:29:19.300 --> 00:29:25.090
A problem that butts up against the United
States' robust free speech protections.

00:29:25.090 --> 00:29:31.940
HOFFMAN: I think what's clear is that the
law hasn't kept pace with technology.

00:29:31.940 --> 00:29:36.690
We're only very belatedly in the aftermath
of the Christchurch massacres, we started

00:29:36.690 --> 00:29:40.610
to talk about it, but those discussions in
many countries hadn't gone very far.

00:29:40.610 --> 00:29:45.450
Now we're presented with the events of January
6, 2021, at the United States Capitol where

00:29:45.450 --> 00:29:52.240
clearly social media played an enormous part,
and even if the mainstream Big Tech companies

00:29:52.240 --> 00:29:57.300
like Facebook or Twitter, for example, removing
this kind of content, it didn't disappear.

00:29:57.300 --> 00:30:02.950
It just migrated to entirely unregulated sites,
especially like Telegram, for example, but

00:30:02.950 --> 00:30:06.270
also to sites like Parler, and Gab, and elsewhere.

00:30:06.270 --> 00:30:09.490
So we really haven't started that discussion.

00:30:09.490 --> 00:30:15.420
The Federal Communications Act section 230,
for instance, excludes social media from many

00:30:15.420 --> 00:30:19.870
of the same restrictions that apply to mainstream
media, to television stations to newspapers

00:30:19.870 --> 00:30:21.640
to radio outlets.

00:30:21.640 --> 00:30:25.580
In an era where most people are getting their
news from social media and not from these

00:30:25.580 --> 00:30:30.570
traditional mainstream sources, one has to
ask whether that's still logical.

00:30:30.570 --> 00:30:31.679
I'm not a jurist.

00:30:31.679 --> 00:30:34.789
I don't have the answer, but we really haven't
had the debate.

00:30:34.789 --> 00:30:40.669
This is a long-overdue discussion and debate
that, of course, is going to receive far greater

00:30:40.669 --> 00:30:43.680
priority in the coming months, and one has
to say years.

00:30:43.680 --> 00:30:47.710
I mean, what we're seeing in the United States
today is not a flash in the pan; emerging

00:30:47.710 --> 00:30:50.809
from this is going to take many years, if
not a decade.

00:30:50.809 --> 00:30:55.700
And of course, this is going to prompt greater
introspection, and I think much more fulsome

00:30:55.700 --> 00:30:57.550
discussion and legislation.

00:30:57.550 --> 00:31:04.670
SIERRA: Looking ahead, these movements may
only seem to be gaining steam, amassing more

00:31:04.670 --> 00:31:09.179
followers who feel emboldened to act on their
hateful ideologies.

00:31:09.179 --> 00:31:17.320
And honestly, as a woman, as a Jewish Puertorican,
as a human being living in this world, it

00:31:17.320 --> 00:31:21.570
is not only unsettling but straight-up terrifying.

00:31:21.570 --> 00:31:27.860
But we have one advantage - the threat has
been laid bare.

00:31:27.860 --> 00:31:34.110
For any skeptics who remained after Charleston,
after the Tree of Life, after Christchurch,

00:31:34.110 --> 00:31:39.020
the image of nationalists storming the Capitol
has put the debate to rest.

00:31:39.020 --> 00:31:43.340
Achieving consensus on this threat is an important
milestone.

00:31:43.340 --> 00:31:48.630
But for a subset of Americans, the Capitol
attack only reinforced a lack of trust in

00:31:48.630 --> 00:31:49.740
government.

00:31:49.740 --> 00:31:54.610
And this mentality continues to be fanned
not only by the networks of online hate but

00:31:54.610 --> 00:32:00.059
also by some mainstream media outlets and
political supporters of former President Donald

00:32:00.059 --> 00:32:04.990
Trump, and even by members of the current
U.S. Congress who go to work every day in

00:32:04.990 --> 00:32:07.159
the building where it happened.

00:32:07.159 --> 00:32:09.970
The road is not going to be smooth.

00:32:09.970 --> 00:32:15.030
The threat is real, and we can never allow
ourselves to return to the time in which we

00:32:15.030 --> 00:32:18.950
ignored it or quibbled about its nature.

00:32:18.950 --> 00:32:23.250
Understanding and teaching the history of
our country's relationship to white supremacy,

00:32:23.250 --> 00:32:28.809
and its long history of racial terror, is
essential to maintaining this clarity.

00:32:28.809 --> 00:32:34.380
As is teaching the history of the foundational
role that immigration and cultural diversity

00:32:34.380 --> 00:32:36.910
play in our country's strength.

00:32:36.910 --> 00:32:41.520
In the past, the United States has demonstrated
its resolve to unite against foreign threats.

00:32:41.520 --> 00:32:48.400
We will now see if it can do so against a
threat to our safety, and our deepest values,

00:32:48.400 --> 00:32:57.470
from within.

